  <p><strong>Can reflective branding go on stretchy leggings or running tops?</strong></p>

  <h2>Specify what you want the logo to do</h2>
  <p>Indicate whether the goal is a design detail or a defined visibility performance requirement. Ask for the intended artwork dimensions and finish. If the graphic is applied across a waistband or high-stretch panel, consider an alternate position and compare both samples.</p>

  <h2>Check the actual fabric rather than the digital mockup</h2>
  <p>A mockup approves layout. A real sample shows whether the edges lift, crack or feel stiff under movement. Check on the intended colour for visual contrast and on the finished garment for distortion. A small successful trial on a different fabric leaves the application on the intended garment unverified.</p>

  <h2>Agree care and documentation before quoting bulk</h2>
  <p>Ask which application process and care instructions the supplier proposes. Agree the number of wash and stretch cycles and what constitutes failure. Record the approved artwork, placement and material so production and inspection refer to the same version.</p>

  <p>Industry reference: <a href="https://www.saksportswear.com/manufacturing">SA K Sportswear presents reflective logos alongside other fabric-dependent embellishment options.</a></p>
